{# table; title: Fey.nvim task list; kind: tasks #}
{# labels, tasks, roadmap #}

  I. About this list

The conversion of the orgmode code to Fey is finished. Its tasks (sections I to IV of the former list: the phases, the Done entries and the summary tables) are in
`TASKS_COMPLETED.fey`, and nothing here needs them to be read. They are an archive; open one only to find out why something was built as it was. This file holds what is
still open, in three sections:

-  V. Open questions:  decisions that are not made yet. Answer one, then delete it from the list and say what was chosen in the docs or the code.
-  VI. Post-rewrite planned features:  wanted, not started. Each is written as a short task: Now (what exists), Fey (how it is spelled), Triggers, Vault, Open.
   When one starts, it gets the full shape of a task and a Done entry when it is finished.
-  VII. Deferred from finished tasks:  what a finished task left undone and is still wanted, one entry per task, named by the task it came from. A letter such as
   "from III.R" refers to `TASKS_COMPLETED.fey`. When a task or a request builds an entry, remove it (the code and the docs are the record). When new work leaves something
   out, add an entry here, not a note in a Done entry.

Rules that still hold: new code, options and text use Fey words (`scripts/terms.lua --check` keeps the old org names out); a task that adds a tag adds its row to the table
IV.A of `TASKS_COMPLETED.fey` (`tests/tags_matrix.lua` reads it); examples of syntax are in fenced blocks, so they stay raw text in this file; `scripts/test.sh` runs every suite.

  II. Open questions

The questions of the proposal were answered by the work and are gone: the sigil convention (the `#` sigil for what is about the text, `@` for what is in it, and the planning
tags are scope tags with the `#` sigil), whether the agenda needs a vault (it reads the court and its hollows, and `fey_agenda_files` only limits a view), one tag or a
positional keyword for the status (the `status` tag, first in the title, keyword and priority together), and the keys of the colliding mappings (`<prefix>yt` for the tangle,
`<prefix>bt` stays the database tab). What is open now:

-  Is a `metadata` grammar field worth the paragraph conflict? The Lua helper (`tags.edit`, the metadata region) has been in use through both phases and was neither too slow
   nor too ambiguous, so the answer is probably no. Decide, then delete the commented rule in `grammar.js` or build it.
-  Should the checkbox items show on the agenda? They are `tasks` rows of kind item with no dates of their own and the headings that hold them are on the agenda already:
   all open items under their heading, only the ones with a date tag of their own, or never.
-  Is the saved agenda view a `TASK` query (VII.D), and does the match language (`+garden-spring/TODO`) then stay only as the prompt, or does one language replace the other?
-  When refile and the heading moves rewrite links (VII.E), do they rewrite signatures or should a linked heading get an `id` prop so that the link needs no rewrite at all? The
   second one makes the notes more stable and a little noisier.
-  Which release removes the deprecated names (the old options, mappings and methods of III.R, `fey_agenda_files`) and the migration that carries them?
-  Is a footnote label local to its file for good (VII.I), or should a hollow share its footnotes?

  III. Post-rewrite planned features

Things that are wanted but not part of converting the org code. They start when the conversion
(III.S) is done, and each is written as a task of the same shape as the ones above when its turn comes.

  III.B. Database view formulas

-  Already supported, nothing to plan. A database has named formulas (`formula.name`) over the
   properties of a note, usable as columns, filters, sorts and groups, added and edited from the view
   (the equals key, `View:add_formula`, `View:edit_formula`), written into the database file under
   `formulas`, and computed by the expression language of the query language. They are part of the
   documentation of the databases in the tutorial of the test vault (section on filters, sorts, groups
   and formulas).
-  Possible later, if wanted: formulas that look at other rows (running totals, rank), summary formulas
   per group, and sharing a formula between databases. Table formulas (built, `tblfm`) use the same
   engine, so a change to one language reaches both.

  III.C. Running source blocks: results and variables

-  Orgmode did not have it either: the plugin tangled and edited blocks but never ran them or read a
   result back, so this was not part of the rewrite (III.I).
-  Wanted later: run a block (`:results`, the output written under the block into a `*_result` pair tag with the
   lifecycle of a query, like III.G), `:var` (values from other blocks, tables and queries), `:session`, and
   noweb across files. Design it with the tags of III.G, and with the executing of code in mind (the
   same care as the `plugin` tag: nothing runs without the user's say).

  III.D. Completion for the query language

-  Completion inside the head of a tag works (III.K) but not for what a `query` tag holds. Wanted: complete the query language
   (`TABLE`, `LIST`, `TASK`, `FROM`, `WHERE`, `SORT`, ..., the fields `file.name`, `file.mtime`, the properties of the notes and the
   functions), the labels after `#`, the files in quotes and the links after `[[`, from the index. Why it matters: a query writes its result with
   links to the files, a `feydb` shows only the data, so tables of contents and indexes of notes should be queries, and completion should
   make writing one easy.

  III.E. Sync the agenda with calendars

-  Wanted later: the agenda and a calendar show the same events. The protocol you were trying to remember is CalDAV (calendars as
   a web service, with the iCalendar format, `.ics`, for the events themselves).
-  Targets: local calendars (KOrganizer or `kcal`, Thunderbird, Evolution, Apple Calendar and Outlook desktop, mostly through `.ics` files or a local CalDAV
   server such as Radicale or vdirsyncer); Google Calendar and Outlook on the web (Microsoft 365), which have their own APIs
   (Google Calendar API, Microsoft Graph) next to CalDAV; and any other online calendar that speaks CalDAV (Nextcloud, Fastmail, iCloud, Zoho).
-  Open questions for the design: one way (calendars into the agenda, read only) first, then both ways (scheduled and deadline dates
   and clocks written out as events, edits coming back); how a calendar event is kept in a note (a `calendar` source with a file per
   calendar, or events only in the index, as dates of a new kind); recurring events against the repeaters of the dates; time zones;
   which credentials, stored where (a keyring, not the notes), and the same care as for the `plugin` tag about what a note may make the
   editor do over the network.

  IV. Deferred from finished tasks

Everything a finished task left undone and that is wanted later lives here, one entry per task, in the order of the tasks, so that a Done entry only says what was
done. Add to it when a task leaves something out. Checked against every "not done" and "not yet" of II and III on 2026-10-07: what a later task or a later request built
is not listed.

  IV.A. Dates (from II.D)
-  The date picker suggests nothing. It was to offer the dates already used in the hollow, which the `dates` table of II.I now makes a query.

  IV.B. Todo state, the `at:` key (from II.E)
-  The optional `at:` key of the status tag (when the state was set).

  IV.C. Hollows and the court (from II.I.i)
-  A command to rename a hollow that rewrites the references to its old id (the links keep the vault part in `links.target_ref`, so the index
   half is there), and `FROM "court:notes"` to name a hollow inside a query (a `scope` does the job today).

  IV.D. Agenda (from II.J and II.K)
-  The scope as a key of the agenda prompt (it is an option, `fey_agenda_scope`, and a key of a custom command and of the API). A saved agenda view written as
   a `TASK` query, a custom command of the type `query`, which needs the items of a result turned into agenda lines that know their place. The match compiled to SQL,
   if a hollow gets too big to filter in Lua.

  IV.E. Refile, archive and the links to a moved heading (from II.N, II.O and II.O.i)
-  II.O.i, which was planned and not started: rewriting the `link` and `section` references to a heading that moves (refile, archive, a subtree move, a renumbering),
   with `fey_refile_rewrite_links` (`ask`, `always`, `never`), one undo step per file, the references found through the backlinks of the index. Today the count is shown and
   the references are left. Refiling a list item subtree. A command that restores an archived heading from its `archived_*` props.

  IV.F. Capture (from II.P)
-  A template key that asks for the labels, the `properties` of a template other than `empty_lines`, capturing a list item, and `whole_file` (accepted and ignored: the
   whole text is always written).

  IV.G. Clock (from III.A)
-  The clock table by label (the per day, per file and per heading tables are there), and the clock report of the agenda over the same code.

  IV.H. Checkboxes (from III.B)
-  The percent done of a heading as a field of the query language, and the checkbox items on the agenda (they are `tasks` rows of kind item, which have no dates
   of their own; see the open questions). The Nerd Font glyphs of the checkbox states were chosen by name and never looked at in a font.

  IV.I. Footnotes (from III.C)
-  Footnotes across files (a label is local to its file), renaming a label together with its references, and the text of a note as virtual text or in a float.

  IV.J. Comments (from III.F)
-  A comment tag in the title of a heading commenting the whole section.

  IV.K. Clock tables (from III.G)
-  `maxlevel` and `tcolumns` of org, and the clock table of one heading's subtree only (`scope: subtree`).

  IV.L. Equations as images (from III.H)
-  Draw `math` as an image, in `images.scm`.

  IV.M. Source blocks (from III.I)
-  Noweb across files (also in VI.C) and block types other than `src`.

  IV.N. Links across hollows (from III.J)
-  The link picker lists only the current hollow, and the broken link report searches only the hollows that are open for `id:` targets.

  IV.O. Completion (from III.K)
-  The text of a tag body (a query, a table cell; the query language is VI.D), the name of a hollow in `scope:`, and the position of a tag in
   the document (a tag directly after a heading, for example).

  IV.P. Highlighting (from III.L)
-  Repaint the overdue dates at midnight with the editor open.

  IV.Q. Leftovers of the old files (from III.N)
-  The unused `find_*` methods of `FeyFile` (one is used by the footnotes) and `fey.buffers`, when nothing uses them.

  IV.R. Export (from III.O)
-  `%{key}` placeholders in the patterns of `tag_exports` (a function does it now), scope tag exports on a title or a table cell, completion of the names
   and forms of `tag_exports`, and a check that warns about a tag name that ends with `_` and is not an HTML element.
-  One file or a site from a whole label or hollow (`Export.label` writes a file per note), resolving `id:` links through the index, styling by `hl`,
   images, and scope tags named like an HTML element (their implied body is the paragraph or list around them, which needs its own rules).

  IV.S. Options (from III.P)
-  The check of the options knows booleans, tag names and a few choices; a schema for every option (numbers, tables, the shape of the nested ones such as `ui`, `notifications`
   and the agenda commands) would catch more.

  IV.T. Tests (from III.Q)
-  Run the suites in parallel in `scripts/test.sh` (about a minute with the screen checks now), a continuous integration workflow that runs it, a measure of which
   lines of Lua no suite reaches, and screen checks for the agenda and the database views (the suites read their text, the screen checks would read what is drawn).

  IV.U. Terminology (from III.R)
-  The words of the agenda views: the view types `tags` and `tags_todo` (and the match language they use) say tags for labels, the agenda filter and the sorting say `tag` for a label, and about
   sixty variables in the agenda, the notifications, refile and capture do the same; rename them with the view types as aliases. The old names of the options and the Lua API are removed
   after a release (`fey.config.migrate`, `fey.utils.deprecate`; which release is an open question).

  IV.V. Documentation (from III.S and II.R)
-  The descriptions of 37 options are missing and many of the others are the wording of the org manual (a "tags" that means labels, "Fey files", agenda files): read `config/_meta.lua` option by option. A web version of the docs
   (the old pages were published at fey.github.io), the screenshots of the tutorial, the page of the Lua API as a generated reference instead of the hand written `fey_api.txt`, and a `query` in the configuration page in place of the generated list.
   The tutorial of the test vault (outside this repository) was brought up to date at II.R and has nothing of phase 2 (the clock table, footnotes, comments, math, source blocks, export, settings tags).

  IV.W. Import (from III.T)
-  Formats beyond the two (reStructuredText, plain HTML, Logseq and Roam pages, Joplin and Notion exports), org's inline footnotes, radio targets, `#+include`, `#+results:` blocks and org links
   to a heading or a custom id of the same file (kept as their text; they could become `section` links once the signatures are known).
-  Markdown: highlights (no Fey form), wikilinks to a heading (`Note#Heading` becomes a link to the note with the heading in the description, because the signature of the heading is
   not known when the file is read; resolving it needs the vault), block ids (`^id`, dropped), embeds of notes (a link, not the transcluded text),
   and a list of one kind next to a list of another kind, which Fey reads as one list.
-  Obsidian plugins (archive import): the Dataview queries and Database Folder views are converted (`fey.import.obsidian`; the folders they read from are found by name in `opts.root`), but `dataviewjs` blocks, Tasks and
   Templater blocks stay source blocks. A dbfolder column keeps its property and width, but the types of the inputs (select options and their colours, checkbox, calendar), the sorting by `sortIndex` and the settings
   of the plugin (cell size, templates folder, pagination) have no place in a database file yet. The Multi-Column plugin (`start-multi-column` fences and the `--- column-end ---` lines) stay as they were: a tag
   that lays text out in columns is to be supported with the export. A Dataview inline field is moved into the table data of the document only before the first heading, and only a field alone on its line.
-  A report mode for a whole folder (a table of the notes, the warnings and the files that did not parse), `--jobs` for large batches, `.fey` files that exist as a reason to skip rather than fail, and rewriting the
   links between the imported files (a link to `a.md` becomes `a.fey` already; a link to a note by its title, the way Obsidian resolves them, does not).
-  tree-sitter-fey: nothing is left of III.T. The unit tests of the scanner (`test/scanner_test.c`) and the stress test (`test/stress_test.py`, it needs the python module `tree_sitter` and tag names without a hyphen) pass again;
   they had drifted from the scanner. A nesting of more than 255 pairs makes more strays than refused openers (a closer that matches no opener may stand for one of the entries the serialized state had to forget).

  IV.X. Tables (from the table formulas)
-  Formulas: org's Calc syntax is read only as far as `$N` (a column), `vsum` and `vmean` and a format after `;` (dropped); references by row (`@2$3`, `@>`), ranges (`$1..$3`), `remote()`, Lisp formulas
   (`'(...)`) and the format specifiers are not, and say so as an error of the table. A formula cannot refer to a table in another note, and a computed cell is not marked: every cell a formula names is
   written again when the table is computed (the tag is the mark). Nothing computes while you type, only the mapping, `fey_table_recalculate_all` and `fey_table_recalculate_on_save`.
-  Alignment: a merged cell takes the alignment of its first column, and the HTML of a merged table has none; a table with no rule row (an org table with no header) cannot hold the marks, so the import
   drops the cookies of org there; Database Folder has no alignment to import. A header that breaks over several lines gets an empty row above it, because the first row of a table cannot be a multi-line row.
-  Width: `tblwidth` is the only setting. The per-cell `minw` and `maxw` of the cell code are still read from nowhere, and the width could be a setting of the note or of the hollow as well as of the table.
